From: Paul Praschl <praschlpaul@g-p.at>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 17:16:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 061/117] dt-bindings: mfd: apple: describe Chestnut PMIC
 register width

The D111 Chestnut PMIC was previously described with apple,i2c-pmic as a
fallback. That generic Apple I2C PMIC compatible selects 16-bit register
addresses, while Chestnut uses an 8-bit register index. A kernel which
does not recognize the more specific device could therefore bind with the
wrong regmap format.

Add a standalone apple,chestnut-pmic compatible and document its register
width alongside the existing Apple PMIC families. Deliberately omit the
incompatible fallback so compatibility cannot silently change the bus
protocol.

The compatible represents a stable PMIC identity; register formatting
remains driver match data rather than a device-tree programming property.
No regmap-core or generic MFD exception is required. The binding passed
dt-doc-validate with dtschema 2026.6, and this patch was checked with
checkpatch.pl --strict.

Signed-off-by: Paul Praschl <praschlpaul@g-p.at>

Orchard-replay-of: 55adbec19bf45f58bfc0445c589047b42c569add
---
 Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mfd/apple,pmic.yaml | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mfd/apple,pmic.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mfd/apple,pmic.yaml
index 25b522995..eb919db3b 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mfd/apple,pmic.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mfd/apple,pmic.yaml
@@ -17,9 +17,10 @@ description: |
   On A7 - A10X SoCs, they are attached over I2C. On A11 and T2 the
   PMIC is attached via SPMI.
 
-  This binding describes simple MFD - I2C/SPMI device with 16-bit
-  register address and 8-bit values that allows subdevices like RTC,
-  power-off control, backlight and nvmem to be described.
+  This binding describes simple MFD - I2C/SPMI devices with 8-bit values
+  that allow subdevices like RTC, power-off control, backlight, regulators
+  and nvmem to be described. Most I2C PMICs use 16-bit register addresses;
+  Chestnut uses 8-bit register addresses.
 
   See also bindings/nvmem/apple,spmi-nvmem.yaml for a simpler
   binding with only nvmem functions exposed used for newer PMICs.
@@ -45,6 +46,7 @@ properties:
               - apple,agnes-pmic
               - apple,calpe-pmic
           - const: apple,spmi-pmic
+      - const: apple,chestnut-pmic
 
   reg:
     maxItems: 1
